WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.050
Good morning John. So in my last video I was
all "I need to make a video about the James

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:07.230
Webb Space Telescope." And then the nerdiest
of the nerds in the comments were all *displays

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.230
comments asking for said video*

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.589
So today, I must talk about the James Webb
Space Telescope because it's freaking awesome!

00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:16.880
But by way of a longer and second introduction
to this topic, I have a thing to say.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.060
I personally believe that there are two ways
to make the world a better place. You can

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.890
decrease the suck, and you can increase the
awesome. Now these are not mutually clexclusive

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.000
things. That's hard to say. Mutually exclusive.
But there are also not the same thing. And

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.640
it's clear that decreasing suck is extremely
important - probably, in the end, more important

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.640
than increasing awesome.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.750
And thus, when I talk about the space program,
people are always like, "NASA's money could

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:44.020
be better spent on services for humanity!"
And to them, I say: I do not want to live

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.080
in a world where we only focus on suck and
never think about awesome.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.800
If we lived in that world, then people would
play soccer by having both teams stand and

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.329
guard their own goal the whole game with the
ball sitting at midfield. And then, at the

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:59.559
end of the game, we're supposed to all celebrate
cause no one scored any goals against our

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.170
team. That's not gonna get you on SportsCenter.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.840
We have to guard the goal, and we have to
try and prevent bad things from happening,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.890
but we also have to make good things happen.
And that is why I love the James Webb Space

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:19.450
Telescope. It's the space telescope version
of - of a freaking awesome...frik- space telescope!

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.040
This thing gives me serious nerd wood.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.270
So without further ado, my nerdy comrades,
here are the top five reasons why the James

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.450
Webb Space Telescope frikkin' kicks ass!

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.810
It is huge! The total meter size is seven
and a half times larger than the Hubble Space

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.799
Telescope's meter. The heat shield is the
size of a tennis court.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:40.399
Number four! It's a freaking transformer.
Because it's so huge, it could never fit in

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:46.579
its full form in the top of a rocket ship.
And so, in order to fit into the rocket, it's

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:53.160
super-duper folded up. And then when it arrives,
it unfolds in like twelve thousand different

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.320
marvelous little ways that could go wrong
at any moment. And it freaks me out, because

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.780
I worry about that. And oh woe for humanity,
no one is going to be there to see this thing

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.240
open up, but luckily, the wonderful computer
animators at NASA have given us the images

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.240
that I have been showing you.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:13.610
Number three! It will operate one million
miles from Earth. That's about four times

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.860
as far away from the Earth as the moon is.
The Webb Telescope actually orbits the Sun,

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.070
that's a simplification. It's actually in
the relative stationary Sun Earth LaGrange

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:28.870
Point Two, which I'm not going to try to explain
to you right now. If it screws up, there's

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.590
no way to fix it. You may have noticed that
we went to fix the Hubble like ten thousand

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.080
different times. Well, we're not gonna get
that opportunity with the James Webb Space

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.260
Telescope. One-off. One chance to do it right,
or it's a six billion dollar piece of space-junk.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.630
That is tense!

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.930
Number two! It will be able see planets orbiting
stars in our galaxy. Individual planets - if

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:55.330
the James Webb Space Telescope was twenty
five light years away, it could see the Earth.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:00.060
It can also determine the chemical composition
of the planets, and it can peer into stellar

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.750
nurseries to see planets as they form, watch
solar systems forming!

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:11.490
And finally, the James Webb Space Telescope
can see thirteen point four billion years

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:17.700
into the past! This is particularly impressive
because the universe is thirteen point seven

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:25.280
billion years old. We will be seeing the first
galaxies as they form, the first stars as

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.280
they form.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.830
And guess what? None of this is necessary
to life on Earth. None of it's going to help

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:37.600
us cure malaria or install a democracy in
Egypt. But it is awesome, in the truest sense

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.600
of the word awesome.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.120
I'm sorry that there's so many hand gestures
in this video. I am feeling very gesture-y.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:51.020
This is the dawn of everything that we know.
And that is increasing awesome. And that is

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.430
something that I can get behind.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.000
John, I'll see you on Friday.

